// wasi:audio-codec — DRAFT / SKETCH (wandr, 2026-06-14, task 108). NOT WIRED.
//
// The OPTIONAL hardware codec lane of the audio stack. Mirrors the W3C
// **WebCodecs** `AudioDecoder` / `AudioEncoder` (EncodedAudioChunk ↔ AudioData,
// isConfigSupported), the audio sibling of the in-tree `wasi:video-codec`.
//
// WHY OPTIONAL (the design's capability-negotiation rule — see
// docs/audio-player-design.md): audio decode is cheap, so the DEFAULT is the
// guest decoding in Rust (Symphonia) and writing PCM through `wasi:audio`
// (the mandatory portable floor). This package is what a guest reaches for to
// OFFLOAD to hardware (battery / DSP) when the host has it. A guest:
//   1. `probe(config)` → hardware | software | unsupported   (= isConfigSupported)
//   2. if acceptable → open + use this; else decode it itself.
// "Use the HW if present, write my own when absent" is one match on the open
// result. A minimal host (or a PipeWire/CoreAudio/WASAPI host with no HW
// codec) advertises `unsupported` and the guest's Rust fallback carries it —
// so the contract stays OS-agnostic. Reuses the `wasi:video-codec` error
// vocabulary so fallback is uniform across media.
//
// TWO OUTPUT TOPOLOGIES (the design's transcode-vs-tunnel decision):
//   • TRANSCODE — decode → PCM returned to the guest via `read` (WebCodecs
//     AudioData). Composable: the guest can EQ / mix / visualize, then write
//     to `wasi:audio`. Used foreground.
//   • TUNNEL — `connect` the decoder to a `wasi:audio` playback sink; decoded
//     PCM flows straight to the device host-side, never crossing back (Android
//     offloaded playback; the CPU sleeps). Used backgrounded/screen-off.
// A good player exposes both and SWITCHES per-situation (`connect` ⇄
// `disconnect`); at any instant one mode is active — tunnel hands the PCM to
// the hardware, so `read` is not simultaneously available.
//
// Cross-package deps (wired, in wit/deps/): `wasi:audio` (the TUNNEL
// `playback` sink) and `wasi:eme` (`decrypt-config` on the chunk + the
// `key-session` bound at `open` for encrypted media).
package wasi:audio-codec@0.0.1;

interface types {
    /// Codecs a host MAY accelerate. The royalty-free set (opus/flac/vorbis/
    /// pcm) is the safe floor; mp3/aac are HW-common but patent-touched, so a
    /// host may decline them and the guest decodes in-process (licensing stays
    /// guest-side, as with the Symphonia feature flags). Discriminants are
    /// ABI-positional and stable — opus=0 … pcm-alaw=6; APPEND only.
    enum codec { opus, aac, mp3, flac, vorbis, pcm-mulaw, pcm-alaw }

    /// HW/SW preference. The first three map to WebCodecs `hardwareAcceleration`
    /// (`no-preference`/`prefer-hardware`/`prefer-software`); `require-hardware` is a
    /// wandr EXTENSION (NOT a WebCodecs value) — the honest fail-rather-than-pay-CPU
    /// opt-out. SHARED VOCABULARY kept byte-IDENTICAL with wasi:video-codec so a
    /// guest's HW/SW fallback is uniform across the A/V pair (candidate to hoist into a
    /// common wasi:media-types once a THIRD consumer appears). For audio, `require-
    /// hardware` is rarely useful (software audio decode is cheap) but stays for parity;
    /// a guest that doesn't need it never passes it. (Appended last: no-preference=0…
    /// require-hardware=3.)
    enum acceleration { no-preference, prefer-hardware, prefer-software, require-hardware }

    /// `probe` result (= WebCodecs isConfigSupported, but explicit about the lane so
    /// the guest can choose rather than have the host hide it). ABI-positional:
    /// hardware=0, software=1, unsupported=2; APPEND only. Shared with wasi:video-codec.
    enum support { hardware, software, unsupported }

    /// Same vocabulary as wasi:video-codec so guest fallback is uniform. ABI-positional:
    /// unsupported-codec=0 … sink-unavailable=5; APPEND only. (Diverges from video by
    /// `bad-data`/`sink-unavailable` vs `bad-frame`/`surface-unavailable` — domain
    /// differences, so the two error enums are deliberately NOT hoisted.)
    enum codec-error {
        unsupported-codec,
        no-hw-codec,
        codec-init-failed,
        bad-data,
        /// Input queue full — retry after draining.
        queue-full,
        /// TUNNEL: no connected sink can accept output; or `read` called in TUNNEL mode.
        sink-unavailable,
    }

    /// WebCodecs `EncodedAudioChunk` (one compressed packet, e.g. demuxed
    /// guest-side or pulled from a network segment).
    ///
    /// ENCRYPTED MEDIA: `decrypt` carries the per-chunk Common-Encryption
    /// params (= WebCodecs `decryptConfig`). The KEY comes from a wasi:eme
    /// `key-session` bound at decoder open; the host decrypts in the secure
    /// path. For a robust CDM the decoder is then SINK-ONLY (TUNNEL) — `read`
    /// is refused so plaintext never reaches the guest; ClearKey MAY allow
    /// `read` (testing). Owner of `decrypt-config` is wasi:eme.
    use wasi:eme/eme@0.0.1.{decrypt-config};
    record encoded-chunk {
        data: list<u8>,
        /// Presentation timestamp in MICROSECONDS (WebCodecs unit).
        timestamp-us: s64,
        /// `key` vs `delta` (= WebCodecs `EncodedAudioChunk.type`). Most audio is
        /// all-key (every AAC/Opus access unit is a sync sample); matters only for the
        /// few codecs with dependent frames.
        keyframe: bool,
        /// Packet duration in microseconds, if the demuxer knows it (WebCodecs-optional).
        /// none = derive from decoded `numberOfFrames / sample-rate`. Useful for gapless
        /// seek accounting.
        duration: option<u64>,
        /// none = cleartext; some = encrypted — decrypt host-side via the
        /// key-session bound at `open`.
        decrypt: option<decrypt-config>,
    }

    /// One block of decoded PCM (= WebCodecs `AudioData`). Interleaved f32 — the one
    /// canonical wire format (the `wasi:audio` PCM format), so no per-buffer format /
    /// planar choice is needed (the guest owns both ends). `samples` is empty until
    /// frames are ready.
    record audio-buffer {
        /// Presentation timestamp of the FIRST sample, microseconds — carried through
        /// from the `encoded-chunk`, in decode order. NEEDED for A/V sync across a seek
        /// or an Opus pre-skip, where accumulating frame counts would drift.
        timestamp-us: s64,
        /// `numberOfFrames × channels` interleaved f32.
        samples: list<f32>,
    }

    /// Constant vs variable bitrate (= WebCodecs `AudioEncoderBitrateMode`, default
    /// `variable`). CBR is needed for constant-rate streaming / RTP.
    enum bitrate-mode { constant, variable }

    /// WebCodecs `AudioDecoderConfig` (subset).
    record decoder-config {
        codec: codec,
        /// Output rate / channel count. `option` because self-describing codecs (Opus
        /// head / FLAC STREAMINFO / AAC esds via `description`) carry them and the
        /// bitstream wins — none = derive. Only headerless `pcm-mulaw`/`pcm-alaw`
        /// REQUIRE them. (The audio analog of video-codec dropping width/height.)
        sample-rate: option<u32>,
        channels: option<u32>,
        acceleration: acceleration,
        /// Codec-private init data (esds / FLAC STREAMINFO / Opus head).
        description: option<list<u8>>,
    }

    /// WebCodecs `AudioEncoderConfig` (subset). Codec-specific dicts (Opus
    /// frame-duration/complexity, AAC format, FLAC block-size) are a DEFERRED subset.
    record encoder-config {
        codec: codec,
        sample-rate: u32,
        channels: u32,
        bitrate-bps: u32,
        /// CBR vs VBR (WebCodecs `bitrateMode`, default variable).
        bitrate-mode: bitrate-mode,
        acceleration: acceleration,
    }
}

interface decoder {
    use types.{decoder-config, encoded-chunk, audio-buffer, support, codec-error};
    use wasi:audio/pcm@0.0.1.{playback};   // TUNNEL sink
    use wasi:eme/eme@0.0.1.{key-session};  // ENCRYPTED MEDIA: the bound keys

    /// = WebCodecs AudioDecoder.isConfigSupported. The guest decides the lane.
    probe: func(config: decoder-config) -> support;

    /// An open HW/SW decode session. Dropping it releases the codec (and, in TUNNEL
    /// mode, unbinds the sink) — ordered teardown is the host's.
    resource audio-decoder {
        /// `keys` binds a wasi:eme key-session (= EME setMediaKeys) for an
        /// encrypted stream; none = cleartext. (The borrow lives in the param,
        /// not `decoder-config` — WIT records can't hold borrows.)
        open: static func(config: decoder-config,
                          keys: option<borrow<key-session>>)
              -> result<audio-decoder, codec-error>;

        /// TUNNEL: bind decoded PCM straight to a wasi:audio playback sink (host-side;
        /// the guest never reads). While connected, `read` returns `sink-unavailable`.
        /// The guest pauses/positions/flushes on the `playback` sink it holds — no codec
        /// verb for that.
        connect: func(sink: borrow<playback>) -> result<_, codec-error>;
        /// Unbind the sink and return to TRANSCODE (`read`) — lets a player switch
        /// foreground-visualizer ⇄ screen-off-offload mid-track without close+reopen (a
        /// HW-codec reopen + audible gap). No-op if not connected.
        disconnect: func();

        /// = WebCodecs decode(EncodedAudioChunk). Push one compressed packet.
        submit: func(chunk: encoded-chunk) -> result<_, codec-error>;

        /// TRANSCODE: drain up to `max-frames` of decoded PCM (an `audio-buffer` with
        /// its PTS). `samples` empty until frames are ready. Returns `sink-unavailable`
        /// if the decoder is in TUNNEL mode.
        ///
        /// POLL, not pollable: returns immediately for the frame-paced reactor model
        /// (the embedder's tick is the wakeup). A `wasi:io/poll` pollable for a
        /// self-scheduling PULL-profile guest (pull PCM, schedule to wasi:audio) is a
        /// deliberate additive deferral — the same split as wasi:surface and
        /// wasi:video-codec's `next-decoded`.
        read: func(max-frames: u32) -> result<audio-buffer, codec-error>;

        /// = WebCodecs flush(): finish decoding queued input (end of stream).
        flush: func();
        /// Seek: discard queued input + carry-over (overlap-add) and return to the
        /// pre-keyframe state, keeping the codec OPEN — avoids a HW-codec reopen on
        /// every seek. Symmetric with wasi:video-codec's `reset`.
        reset: func() -> result<_, codec-error>;
    }
}

interface encoder {
    use types.{encoder-config, encoded-chunk, support, codec-error};

    probe: func(config: encoder-config) -> support;

    /// An open HW/SW encode session; dropping it releases the codec.
    resource audio-encoder {
        open: static func(config: encoder-config)
              -> result<audio-encoder, codec-error>;

        /// = WebCodecs encode(AudioData). Push interleaved f32 frames.
        submit: func(samples: list<f32>) -> result<_, codec-error>;

        /// Drain finished compressed packets (= the EncodedAudioChunk output). POLL,
        /// not pollable — same rationale as the decoder's `read` (pollable deferred).
        read: func() -> list<encoded-chunk>;

        /// Samples-per-channel per output packet (AAC 1024, Opus 20 ms @ rate, …). The
        /// guest needs it to feed exactly one frame for low-latency call encode and to
        /// stamp each output chunk's `duration` when muxing.
        frame-size: func() -> u32;

        /// First-packet codec-private bytes (esds / Opus head) once available. Present
        /// here (unlike the video encoder) because audio codec-private init is
        /// out-of-band, where H.26x SPS/PPS ride in-band in the bitstream.
        description: func() -> option<list<u8>>;

        flush: func();
    }
}

world audio-codec-guest {
    import types;
    import decoder;
    import encoder;
}
